WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.104 --> 00:00:04.904
Easy.

00:00:06.306 --> 00:00:07.507
Don't, don't.

00:00:14.381 --> 00:00:15.315
And cut.

00:00:15.715 --> 00:00:16.549
Hi, I'm Caroline.

00:00:16.549 --> 00:00:18.418
I'm at House of Moves in Los Angeles,

00:00:18.418 --> 00:00:21.321
and today I'm gonna learn how
to act for motion capture.

00:00:21.321 --> 00:00:22.355
I'm gonna get suited up.

00:00:22.355 --> 00:00:24.290
I don't know what I'm
gonna look like on-screen,

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:25.625
but let's find out.

00:00:26.726 --> 00:00:29.429
House of Moves has been
the stage for many projects

00:00:29.429 --> 00:00:33.133
in TV, film, and video
games that involve motion

00:00:33.133 --> 00:00:34.901
and performance capture.

00:00:34.901 --> 00:00:37.370
I've seen plenty of
videos of actors wearing

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:40.407
these suits covered in
dots or Ping-Pong balls.

00:00:40.407 --> 00:00:44.177
Did I have what it takes to
be the next Caesar or Alita?

00:00:44.744 --> 00:00:47.347
To coach me through it, we
met with one of the best

00:00:47.347 --> 00:00:49.749
in the business, Richard Dorton.

00:00:49.749 --> 00:00:52.986
He's a professional
motion-capture actor and a teacher

00:00:52.986 --> 00:00:54.287
at The Mocap Vaults.

00:00:54.287 --> 00:00:56.523
His résumé is extensive.

00:00:57.457 --> 00:00:58.758
Richard: If you've played a video game,

00:00:58.758 --> 00:01:00.560
you've probably killed me.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.962
Caroline: He gave me some
valuable acting tips,

00:01:02.962 --> 00:01:05.031
and then I put them to the test.

00:01:10.336 --> 00:01:12.939
But first, a little background on motion

00:01:12.939 --> 00:01:14.474
and performance capture.

00:01:15.341 --> 00:01:18.278
Richard: Motion capture came
out of the medical industry.

00:01:18.278 --> 00:01:21.181
They were capturing the moves of peoples

00:01:21.181 --> 00:01:23.383
walks, their gaits, they were studying it

00:01:23.383 --> 00:01:24.818
for medical reasons.

00:01:24.818 --> 00:01:27.921
As it moved into the
entertainment industry,

00:01:27.921 --> 00:01:29.422
motion capture was used for

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:31.758
video-game characters.

00:01:31.758 --> 00:01:34.727
And performance capture, that grew out of

00:01:34.727 --> 00:01:36.396
"The Polar Express," the
movie with Tom Hanks.

00:01:36.396 --> 00:01:38.031
He coined the phrase "performance capture"

00:01:38.031 --> 00:01:41.668
because we were now capturing
the body with the face

00:01:41.668 --> 00:01:43.036
and with the voice.

00:01:43.036 --> 00:01:45.638
Caroline: Motion and performance
capture are responsible

00:01:45.638 --> 00:01:47.807
for some amazing performances.

00:01:53.146 --> 00:01:56.149
Now it was my turn to get suited up.

00:01:56.149 --> 00:01:58.818
It's not itchy or anything,
so it's pretty comfortable.

00:01:58.818 --> 00:02:01.020
It's kinda like your workout clothes.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:01.921
This is fun.

00:02:03.123 --> 00:02:06.493
I was covered head-to-toe
in these reflective markers,

00:02:06.493 --> 00:02:09.195
which are actually made
from ground-up glass.

00:02:09.195 --> 00:02:11.564
They'll help capture movement
from all different parts

00:02:11.564 --> 00:02:13.133
of my body.

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:15.101
Why do I feel like a mannequin?

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:19.439
Through some sort of
miracle of technology,

00:02:19.439 --> 00:02:21.841
I was going to be transformed
into one of the creatures

00:02:21.841 --> 00:02:25.145
from the 2016 online game "Paragon."

00:02:25.145 --> 00:02:28.481
I would be a character named
Grux, and this soldier.

00:02:28.481 --> 00:02:31.584
But first, I had to get a full-body scan.

00:02:31.584 --> 00:02:32.886
Motion-capture artist: Lean forward.

00:02:32.886 --> 00:02:34.287
Lean back.

00:02:34.287 --> 00:02:35.388
Side-to-side.

00:02:36.422 --> 00:02:37.790
Twist.

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:39.225
Twist.

00:02:39.225 --> 00:02:40.660
Reach up to the sky.

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:43.329
Richard: Your range of motion,
we do this so that we can,

00:02:43.329 --> 00:02:45.265
when we put the animated
character on top of you,

00:02:45.265 --> 00:02:48.301
we can see that it has
a full range of motion.

00:02:48.301 --> 00:02:50.837
It's just basically getting the system

00:02:50.837 --> 00:02:52.672
to recognize your skeleton.

00:02:52.672 --> 00:02:56.176
In the game industry, we
have to fake everything,

00:02:56.176 --> 00:02:58.545
and motion capture's all
about pure imagination.

00:02:58.545 --> 00:03:01.014
So you have to imagine the environment.

00:03:01.014 --> 00:03:03.149
You have to create the
environment with your body.

00:03:03.149 --> 00:03:05.952
Caroline: And with that, it
was time to start faking.

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:13.927
The scene: a dark, grimy subway station.

00:03:13.927 --> 00:03:15.495
I started off as Grux.

00:03:15.495 --> 00:03:19.199
Needless to say, Grux doesn't
look anything like me.

00:03:19.199 --> 00:03:21.534
Richard: So for Grux, we want

00:03:21.534 --> 00:03:24.137
this big, hulking character, right?

00:03:24.137 --> 00:03:27.407
But we've now gotta bring
the weight and the heaviness

00:03:27.407 --> 00:03:30.443
to this character 'cause
he's vicious character.

00:03:30.443 --> 00:03:33.880
So it's all about how you center yourself,

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:35.315
how you center your weight.

00:03:35.315 --> 00:03:36.816
Walking is really important.

00:03:36.816 --> 00:03:39.852
We wanna figure out how,

00:03:39.852 --> 00:03:40.687
how he moves.

00:03:40.687 --> 00:03:42.622
How we can stay center.

00:03:43.623 --> 00:03:44.457
And

00:03:45.491 --> 00:03:46.793
you're holding your breath.

00:03:46.793 --> 00:03:47.627
Yeah.

00:03:48.761 --> 00:03:49.996
You need to breathe.

00:03:49.996 --> 00:03:52.765
Your breath should help...
I'm nervous.

00:03:52.765 --> 00:03:55.301
It's gonna help create
this character, you know?

00:03:55.301 --> 00:03:56.636
Caroline: It was a little nerve-racking

00:03:56.636 --> 00:03:58.471
at first, but after a while,

00:03:58.471 --> 00:04:00.807
I started to get the hang of it.

00:04:00.807 --> 00:04:02.842
So I should breathe heavily.
How does he breathe?

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.582
Caroline: Dorton told me to
find the animal that Grux

00:04:09.582 --> 00:04:12.185
is most similar to and try to imitate

00:04:12.185 --> 00:04:14.621
what that real-life animal is like.

00:04:14.621 --> 00:04:16.222
We landed on a rhino.

00:04:17.123 --> 00:04:20.760
Once I could walk like a
mocap monster, I had to adjust

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:22.161
to holding weapons.

00:04:22.161 --> 00:04:24.330
So let's give you something to swing.

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:25.131
Oh.

00:04:26.432 --> 00:04:28.001
OK, wow, I'm sweating.

00:04:29.602 --> 00:04:33.039
And now, because these are
lighter weight, lightweight,

00:04:33.039 --> 00:04:35.074
you have to bring the weight to them.

00:04:35.074 --> 00:04:36.576
You have to make them heavy.

00:04:36.576 --> 00:04:39.212
But you're a big, you're a
big creature, so it's gonna...

00:04:39.212 --> 00:04:41.681
I have zero arm strength,
so they're pretty heavy.

00:04:41.681 --> 00:04:43.082
This is good.

00:04:43.082 --> 00:04:44.984
But let's just take a,

00:04:44.984 --> 00:04:46.853
let's just take a swing to the right.

00:04:49.022 --> 00:04:49.856
And to the left.

00:04:52.358 --> 00:04:54.327
Caroline: Maybe the most important part,

00:04:54.327 --> 00:04:56.029
a trademark roar.

00:04:56.929 --> 00:04:57.764
Oh, OK.

00:04:57.764 --> 00:04:58.631
You gotta have your

00:05:02.235 --> 00:05:03.102
big growl.

00:05:05.038 --> 00:05:06.139
It's gonna be loud.

00:05:06.139 --> 00:05:07.740
That's why we're here.

00:05:21.688 --> 00:05:24.490
We just have to find
where, where this monster

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:25.391
is inside of you.

00:05:25.391 --> 00:05:27.760
You're a very happy, nice person.

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:30.596
You've gotta get angry. You've
gotta get a little tough.

00:05:30.596 --> 00:05:32.365
You gotta get a little meaner.

00:05:45.378 --> 00:05:46.979
Caroline: Once I felt more comfortable

00:05:46.979 --> 00:05:50.650
and found a character
motivation, I was ready to fight!

00:06:18.111 --> 00:06:20.113
Richard: What the hell is that?

00:06:22.882 --> 00:06:23.716
Easy.

00:06:25.284 --> 00:06:26.119
Don't, don't, don't make, please don't.

00:06:27.253 --> 00:06:30.923
Caroline: [Growling]

00:06:39.432 --> 00:06:43.202
Richard: So creating that kind
of monster, heavy characters,

00:06:43.202 --> 00:06:45.338
that's one thing that we do.

00:06:45.338 --> 00:06:48.474
Now, you have to be a believable person.

00:06:48.474 --> 00:06:51.310
OK, well now I'm gonna be the soldier.

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:52.745
God, I'm losing my voice already.

00:06:52.745 --> 00:06:54.614
I really got into character there.

00:06:56.015 --> 00:06:57.450
Caroline: There were some key differences

00:06:57.450 --> 00:06:58.851
to playing the soldier.

00:06:58.851 --> 00:07:01.521
This time, my breaths had to be quieter.

00:07:01.521 --> 00:07:04.090
Rather than being big and intimidating,

00:07:04.090 --> 00:07:08.327
my body movements needed
to express plausible fear.

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:16.035
Richard: [Growling]

00:07:35.621 --> 00:07:38.057
And up, T-pose.

00:07:39.459 --> 00:07:42.428
Caroline: The real key to
giving a great mocap performance

00:07:42.428 --> 00:07:44.664
is actually pretty straightforward.

00:07:45.565 --> 00:07:46.899
Richard: The technology has really

00:07:46.899 --> 00:07:49.035
helped performance capture.

00:07:49.035 --> 00:07:50.803
There were certain things
we couldn't do early on.

00:07:50.803 --> 00:07:53.339
We couldn't cross our
arms because the markers

00:07:53.339 --> 00:07:54.640
would get blocked.

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:57.243
I tell my students, "Don't
let the technology interfere

00:07:57.243 --> 00:07:59.579
with your performance.
That's not your job.

00:07:59.579 --> 00:08:02.281
Your job is to perform,
and the postproduction,

00:08:02.281 --> 00:08:05.117
it's their job to fix it if they need to."

00:08:05.117 --> 00:08:06.819
Caroline: This was awesome.

00:08:06.819 --> 00:08:09.922
This was unlike anything
I've ever tried before.

00:08:09.922 --> 00:08:11.557
I am exhausted.

00:08:11.557 --> 00:08:14.126
I'm losing my voice from all the growling

00:08:14.126 --> 00:08:16.295
and all the movements that I was doing.

00:08:16.295 --> 00:08:18.331
But once you kinda get into the character,

00:08:18.331 --> 00:08:21.434
you feel like it's, it comes
like second nature to you.

00:08:21.434 --> 00:08:23.703
With, like, you don't feel
like you're wearing a suit

00:08:23.703 --> 00:08:25.471
or anything, and, like, your weapons

00:08:25.471 --> 00:08:28.040
become your actual weapons.

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:30.209
I think the most important
thing Richard taught me

00:08:30.209 --> 00:08:34.580
was to really get in character
and just imagine everything.

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:35.882
Like imagine where I am,

00:08:35.882 --> 00:08:39.118
imagine where the character
is, and just go for it.

00:08:40.152 --> 00:08:41.354
This is my runway walk.

